Title: "Logic"
Id: "2675f869-5535-45d3-9009-0a76ffa07961"


Text: "Mathematics begins with assertion. A mathematical statement—often called
       a *proposition*—is a declarative sentence that is definitively either
       true or false, but never both, never neither, and never indeterminate.
       This classical ideal, known as the *principle of bivalence*, provides the
       bedrock upon which mathematical reasoning stands. We do not require that
       we immediately *know* whether a given proposition is true or
       false—Goldbach's conjecture and the Riemann hypothesis are propositions
       whose truth values remain actively pursued—only that a definite truth
       value exists independently of our knowledge.

       To build complex mathematical theories from elementary observations, we
       require a systematic grammar for combining and transforming propositions.
       This branch of foundational mathematics is *propositional logic*. In this
       setting, atomic statements are treated as indivisible units, abstracted
       as variables $P, Q, R, \ldots$. By applying a small collection of
       truth-functional connectives, we can assemble compound statements whose
       truth or falsity is completely determined by the truth values of their
       constituent parts.

       The logical connectives formulated in this chapter are entirely
       topic-neutral: they operate according to universal rules that apply
       equally whether the underlying assertions concern prime numbers,
       topological spaces, continuous functions, or abstract algebraic groups.
       They are the universal syntax of mathematical discourse."
Id: "b03f7ce2-7aed-4c81-a6e7-d998ccf65747"


SectionTitle: "Conditionals and Equivalence"
Id: "706c5ccb-8690-478e-b0fd-90e906f0276e"


Text: "The primary connective of mathematical deduction is the *conditional* or
       *material implication*, denoted $P \Rightarrow Q$ and read *if $P$, then
       $Q$*. Virtually every mathematical theorem is cast in this mold: given a
       collection of hypotheses $P$, we establish that a conclusion $Q$
       necessarily follows.

       In colloquial speech, saying 'if it rains, I will bring an umbrella'
       suggests a causal or temporal link. In mathematical logic, however, the
       conditional is purely *truth-functional*: its truth value depends solely
       on the truth values of $P$ and $Q$, free of any requirement of
       psychological or causal connection. The conditional $P \Rightarrow Q$
       makes a very precise claim: it is violated if and only if the hypothesis
       $P$ is true while the conclusion $Q$ is false.

       Consequently, whenever the antecedent $P$ is false, the conditional
       $P \Rightarrow Q$ is automatically regarded as true. This convention is
       known as *vacuous truth*. If a mathematician promises, 'Every element of
       the empty set is an even integer,' the assertion is true because there
       are no counterexamples—the premise that an element belongs to the empty
       set is unconditionally false. Vacuous truth is not an arbitrary quirk of
       formalization; it is essential for mathematical consistency, guaranteeing
       that general laws apply uniformly to boundary cases without requiring
       countless ad hoc exceptions."
Id: "7529a4ce-e5cc-46c7-85b9-fdcd19351351"


[P \.implies./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. if: P
  then: Q
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ implies $Q?$"
. written: "P? \Rightarrow Q?"
. description: "The conditional *if $P$ then $Q$*, written $P \Rightarrow Q$. It
                asserts that whenever $P$ holds, $Q$ holds as well. It makes no
                claim at all in the case that $P$ fails, so a conditional with a
                false hypothesis is regarded as holding vacuously."
Id: "dc7e213d-76c2-43d7-acf1-c5978daaab33"


Text: "A one-way implication $P \Rightarrow Q$ tells us that $P$ is a
       *sufficient condition* for $Q$, and that $Q$ is a *necessary condition*
       for $P$. Frequently in mathematics, however, two conditions are so
       intimately linked that each implies the other. When both
       $P \Rightarrow Q$ and $Q \Rightarrow P$ hold, the statements $P$ and $Q$
       stand or fall together.

       This two-way implication is the *biconditional*, written
       $P \Leftrightarrow Q$ and read *$P$ if and only if $Q$* (often
       abbreviated as *$P$ iff $Q$*). The biconditional asserts that $P$ and $Q$
       have identical truth values: both are true, or both are false. It is the
       logical expression of *equivalence*. Most mathematical definitions are
       implicit biconditionals: to say that a triangle is equilateral if and
       only if all three of its sides have equal length is to establish a
       complete mutual equivalence between the concept named and the condition
       characterizing it."
Id: "b60eca2f-6be8-4e23-a2f2-b56e2378684b"


[P \.iff./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. have: P
  iff: Q
Documented:
. called:
  . "$P?$ if and only if $Q?$"
  . "$P?$ iff $Q?$"
. written: "P? \iff Q?"
. description: "The biconditional of $P$ and $Q$, read *$P$ if and only if $Q$*.
                It holds exactly when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value --
                each implies the other -- and it is the connective used to state
                that two conditions are equivalent."
Id: "96440ac7-3331-4467-a7ac-593dbca0761b"


SectionTitle: "Boolean Connectives"
Id: "faacb189-b227-404c-8a8a-b6e976fc828a"


Text: "In 1854, George Boole demonstrated that the laws of thought could be
       expressed as an algebra: propositions could be added, multiplied, and
       negated according to precise mathematical rules. The three primary
       *Boolean connectives*—conjunction ($\land$), disjunction ($\lor$), and
       negation ($\neg$)—form the computational engine of propositional logic.
       Together, they allow us to assemble arbitrarily complex truth-functional
       statements from atomic parts.

       Conjunction represents the logical 'and': a compound statement
       $P \land Q$ is true if and only if both $P$ and $Q$ are simultaneously
       true. In algebraic analogies, conjunction behaves like multiplication in
       the two-element field $\{0, 1\}$: the product is $1$ only when both
       factors are $1$."
Id: "1d61c6bd-bf4c-4c3e-a6dd-769d5c662a6f"


[P \.and./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. allOf:
  . P
  . Q
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ and $Q?$"
. written: "P? \land Q?"
. description: "The conjunction of $P$ and $Q$. The statement $P \text{ and } Q$
                holds precisely when $P$ holds and $Q$ holds; if either fails,
                the conjunction fails."
Id: "e024d041-fcf1-476e-b29e-b75d88b04fa8"


Text: "Disjunction represents the logical 'or', written $P \lor Q$. In natural
       language, the word 'or' is notoriously ambiguous: a menu offering 'soup
       or salad' implies an exclusive choice, whereas a job requirement stating
       'applicants must speak French or German' clearly welcomes applicants who
       speak both.

       Mathematics resolves this ambiguity decisively: unless explicitly
       qualified, the mathematical connective $\lor$ is always *inclusive*. The
       statement $P \lor Q$ is true when $P$ is true, when $Q$ is true, or when
       both are true; it fails only when both $P$ and $Q$ are false. This
       inclusive convention is indispensable in mathematical reasoning. For
       example, the theorem stating that for every real number $x$, either
       $x \ge 0$ or $x \le 0$, is valid without awkward qualifications for
       $x = 0$ precisely because the connective permits both alternatives to
       hold at once."
Id: "fd7a79c3-4380-4621-a699-eb6902667669"


[P \.or./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. anyOf:
  . P
  . Q
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ or $Q?$"
. written: "P? \lor Q?"
. description: "The disjunction of $P$ and $Q$. The statement $P \text{ or } Q$
                holds when at least one of $P$ and $Q$ holds. As is standard in
                mathematics, the *or* is inclusive: it also holds when both do."
Id: "f401f869-7009-411a-bc01-9a76ffa07901"


Text: "The final primary connective is *negation*, written $\neg P$ and read
       *not $P$*. Unlike conjunction and disjunction, which are binary
       operations combining two arguments, negation is a unary operation acting
       on a single statement. It acts as an involution on truth values: if $P$
       is true, $\neg P$ is false; if $P$ is false, $\neg P$ is true. Under
       classical bivalence, asserting $\neg P$ is entirely equivalent to
       asserting that $P$ fails to hold."
Id: "856ad9cc-c17e-4cff-ad32-10ee78ab20c4"


[\not{P}]
States:
when: P is \\statement
that:
. not: P
Documented:
. called: "not $P?$"
. written: "\neg P+?"
. description: "The negation of $P$, written $\neg P$. It holds exactly when $P$
                does not, and so reverses the truth value of the statement it is
                applied to."
Id: "c28b5247-41fe-455b-80df-261056c1eb02"


Text: "Although the trio $\{\neg, \land, \lor\}$ is more than adequate to
       express every truth function, it is often conceptually clarifying to
       introduce specialized connectives for common patterns of thought.

       When we wish to capture the everyday 'either-or but not both', we use the
       *exclusive disjunction*, written $P \oplus Q$ or $P \text{ xor } Q$. An
       exclusive disjunction holds when the two statements have opposite truth
       values. Algebraically, it represents the symmetric difference in set
       theory and addition modulo $2$ in the field $\mathbb{F}_2$."
Id: "009e13c8-8dba-4b31-9958-f8531eb5ec04"


[P \.xor./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that: (P \.or./ Q) \.and./ \not{P \.and./ Q}
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ xor $Q?$"
. written: "P? \text{ xor } Q?"
. description: "The exclusive disjunction of $P$ and $Q$. It holds when $P$ or
                $Q$ holds but *not* both, and is defined here as $(P \text{ or }
                Q) \text{ and } \neg(P \text{ and } Q)$ -- inclusive *or* with
                the both-true case removed."
Id: "1c7bc638-0db8-416f-a0d0-f5c0d7d92842"


Text: "From the standpoint of expressive economy, we may ask: what is the minimal
       collection of connectives needed to generate all possible Boolean functions?
       While neither conjunction alone nor disjunction alone suffices, Charles
       Sanders Peirce (around 1880) and Henry Sheffer (1913) independently
       discovered that a single binary connective can generate the entire system of
       logic.

       The two canonical operators with this property are *alternative denial*
       (the Sheffer stroke or $\text{NAND}$, written $\barwedge$) and *joint denial*
       (the Peirce arrow or $\text{NOR}$, written $\downarrow$). Because
       $P \barwedge P \Leftrightarrow \neg P$ and $(P \barwedge Q) \barwedge
       (P \barwedge Q) \Leftrightarrow P \land Q$, a logician or circuit designer
       can build all of classical propositional calculus using NAND gates alone."
Id: "2d5efb63-58bb-436f-9aa6-648a2c05e6b6"


[P \.nand./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that: \not{P \.and./ Q}
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ nand $Q?$"
. written: "P? \barwedge Q?"
. description: "The alternative denial (Sheffer stroke) of $P$ and $Q$. It is
                true if and only if at least one of the propositions is false,
                failing only when both $P$ and $Q$ are simultaneously true."
Id: "ac552ed6-48a1-4639-940a-9d9a291c76e4"


[P \.nor./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that: \not{P \.or./ Q}
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ nor $Q?$"
. written: "P? \downarrow Q?"
. description: "The joint denial (Peirce arrow) of $P$ and $Q$. It asserts that
                neither $P$ nor $Q$ holds, being true precisely when both
                statements are false."
Id: "a16193ad-6084-494c-afd2-b4f7c6a70a7d"


SectionTitle: "Fundamental Laws of Propositional Logic"
Id: "1b3ef4fc-1add-407c-84c2-ac7234d8e0f9"


Text: "Under the relation of logical equivalence ($\Leftrightarrow$),
       propositions satisfy a rich system of algebraic identities. In fact, when
       propositions are identified modulo equivalence, the resulting
       mathematical structure is a *Boolean algebra*—an algebraic system sharing
       deep structural parallels with the algebra of sets under intersection,
       union, and complementation.

       The first foundational law is the *law of double negation*: denying a
       denial restores the original assertion. In classical logic, where every
       statement is either true or false, there is no third epistemic status;
       hence, to demonstrate that it is false that $P$ is false is to establish
       that $P$ is true. This equivalence underpins the method of proof by
       contradiction."
Id: "ddf66eda-3e4a-4b16-a628-eaa5d5dbc270"


Theorem:
given: P is \\statement
then: \not{\not{P}} \.iff./ P
Documented:
. called: "Law of Double Negation"
. description: "A proposition $P$ is logically equivalent to the negation of its
                negation $\neg(\neg P)$."
Proof: """Under classical bivalence, the statement {.P.} has two possible truth
          values. If {.P.} is true, its negation {.\not{P}.} is false, and the
          negation of that result {.\not{\not{P}}.} is true. Conversely, if
          {.P.} is false, {.\not{P}.} is true, and {.\not{\not{P}}.} is false.
          In both cases, {.\not{\not{P}}.} has the identical truth value as
          {.P.}. Therefore, {.\not{\not{P}} \.iff./ P.}."""
Id: "eb21571a-d245-427d-a014-d29407d09f9a"


Text: "Next in importance are *De Morgan's laws*, named after the British
       mathematician Augustus De Morgan (1806–1871). These laws reveal a
       profound duality between conjunction and disjunction mediated by
       negation.

       De Morgan's laws state that negating a conjunction turns it into a
       disjunction of negations, while negating a disjunction turns it into a
       conjunction of negations: $$\neg(P \land Q) \iff (\neg P \lor \neg Q)$$
       $$\neg(P \lor Q) \iff (\neg P \land \neg Q)$$

       The intuitive meaning is direct: to say that 'it is not the case that
       both $P$ and $Q$ hold' means that at least one of them must fail;
       similarly, to say that 'neither $P$ nor $Q$ holds' means that $P$ fails
       and $Q$ fails. These laws appear in every domain of mathematics: in set
       theory, as the laws governing complements of unions and intersections
       ($(A \cup B)^c = A^c \cap B^c$), and in predicate calculus, as the
       duality between universal and existential quantifiers
       ($\neg \forall x P(x) \iff \exists x \neg P(x)$)."
Id: "2f552add-aa29-4824-b7d0-40345e29c312"


Theorem:
given: P, Q is \\statement
then:
. \not{P \.and./ Q} \.iff./ (\not{P} \.or./ \not{Q})
. \not{P \.or./ Q} \.iff./ (\not{P} \.and./ \not{Q})
Documented:
. called: "De Morgan's Laws for Logic"
. description: "Negation interchanges conjunction and disjunction: $\neg(P \land Q)
                \iff (\neg P \lor \neg Q)$ and $\neg(P \lor Q) \iff (\neg P \land \neg Q)$."
Proof: """We evaluate the statements under all four assignments of truth values
          to {.P.} and {.Q.}:

          1. If both {.P.} and {.Q.} are true, then {.P \.and./ Q.} is true, so
             {.\not{P \.and./ Q}.} is false. Meanwhile, {.\not{P}.} and
             {.\not{Q}.} are both false, so {.\not{P} \.or./ \not{Q}.} is false.
             Both sides agree.

          2. If at least one of {.P.} or {.Q.} is false, then {.P \.and./ Q.} is
             false, so {.\not{P \.and./ Q}.} is true. At the same time, at least
             one of {.\not{P}.} or {.\not{Q}.} is true, so
             {.\not{P} \.or./ \not{Q}.} is true. Again, both sides agree.

          The dual identity
          {.\not{P \.or./ Q} \.iff./ (\not{P} \.and./ \not{Q}).} follows by
          applying the first identity to {.\not{P}.} and {.\not{Q}.} together
          with the law of double negation."""
Id: "e4c6ef38-dd37-448b-a1f5-377df32138ce"


SectionTitle: "Truth Assignments and Expressive Power"
Id: "1fba02b7-9700-4256-a888-1a44c75c3c02"


Text: "Having defined individual connectives and their algebraic laws, we now
       turn to the overarching semantic framework of propositional logic. We
       wish to understand how the truth value of an arbitrary, arbitrarily
       nested formula is determined by the values of its atomic variables, and
       what totality of functions can be expressed within this syntax."
Id: "3e84617a-51ea-42ce-9065-27a36e676101"


TextDefinition: "A *truth assignment* (or valuation) on a set of propositional
                 variables is a function mapping each variable to one of the
                 values $\{\text{true}, \text{false}\}$. Any truth assignment
                 extends uniquely and recursively to all compound formulas
                 according to the truth tables of their connectives.

                 A formula is called a *tautology* (or logically valid) if it
                 evaluates to true under every possible truth assignment; it is
                 a *contradiction* (or unsatisfiable) if it evaluates to false
                 under every assignment; and it is *contingent* if it is true
                 under some assignments and false under others. Tautologies
                 represent universal logical truths, independent of any factual
                 state of affairs."
Id: "e6a57aaf-f2e1-43ce-9979-261056c1eba7"


Text: "A central question of mathematical syntax is *canonical representation*:
       can an arbitrary propositional formula be rewritten in a standard, easily
       analyzable form? The answer is provided by the normal form theorems."
Id: "81e3a647-38ae-4f70-b184-a134d193d501"


TextTheorem: "Every propositional formula is logically equivalent to one in
              *Disjunctive Normal Form* (DNF) and to one in *Conjunctive Normal
              Form* (CNF).

              A formula in DNF is a disjunction of conjunctions of literals
              (variables or their negations), analogous to a sum-of-products in
              elementary algebra. It can be constructed directly from the
              formula's truth table by taking a conjunction for each row where
              the formula evaluates to true, and forming the disjunction of
              these conjunctions.

              Dually, a formula in CNF is a conjunction of disjunctions of
              literals (a product-of-sums). Although the transformation into
              either normal form can produce an exponential increase in formula
              length, the normal forms guarantee that every Boolean truth
              function admits a completely explicit, canonical representation."
Id: "a415c422-9e69-431e-b2fe-8a833231b229"


Text: "Finally, we record the foundational theorem regarding the expressive
       power of minimal connective sets."
Id: "5b736e47-8a4d-451e-9a18-971c26a57891"


TextTheorem: "The singleton connective sets consisting only of NAND
              ($\{\barwedge\}$) or only of NOR ($\{\downarrow\}$) are
              *functionally complete*: every Boolean truth function of any
              number of variables can be expressed using formulas containing
              that single connective alone.

              Functional completeness guarantees that our logical universe lacks
              nothing in expressive reach. In subsequent chapters, when we speak
              of mathematical definitions, properties, and constraints, we do so
              with the certainty that any finite truth condition can be
              formulated rigorously within the language of propositional logic."
Id: "e5bdaf8f-ebd3-4925-ac4c-5e7cad6e9f64"
